1
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:04,868
September first, nineteen seventy-three.

2
00:00:04,908 --> 00:00:08,258
Four hundred and eighty metres beneath the
Atlantic,

3
00:00:08,298 --> 00:00:13,538
two men are sitting in the dark inside a steel
sphere that is running out of air.

4
00:00:13,578 --> 00:00:15,103
Above them, the surface.

5
00:00:15,143 --> 00:00:17,971
Outside them, the weight of the whole ocean.

6
00:00:18,011 --> 00:00:21,752
When the hatch finally opens, the engineers will
check the

7
00:00:21,792 --> 00:00:24,881
cylinder and find twelve minutes of oxygen left.

8
00:00:24,921 --> 00:00:27,619
This is the deepest rescue ever completed.

9
00:00:27,659 --> 00:00:32,639
And it began with something so ordinary that
nobody on the ship looked twice.

10
00:00:32,679 --> 00:00:36,811
Three days earlier, the morning of August
twenty-ninth was calm.

11
00:00:36,851 --> 00:00:40,201
Two hundred and forty kilometres south-west of
Cork,

12
00:00:40,241 --> 00:00:45,612
the cable ship Vickers Voyager was laying a
transatlantic telephone line across the

13
00:00:45,652 --> 00:00:46,252
seabed.

14
00:00:46,108 --> 00:00:48,872
The work was slow and muddy and repetitive.

15
00:00:48,912 --> 00:00:52,392
A submersible crawls along the bottom at walking
pace,

16
00:00:52,432 --> 00:00:57,021
blasting jets of water into the ooze, then
burying the cable behind it.

17
00:00:57,061 --> 00:00:59,628
Two men made that dive, again and again.

18
00:00:59,668 --> 00:01:01,454
Roger Chapman, twenty-eight.

19
00:01:01,494 --> 00:01:03,344
Roger Mallinson, thirty-five.

20
00:01:03,384 --> 00:01:04,583
Both professionals.

21
00:01:04,623 --> 00:01:07,100
Both, by that morning, a little tired.

22
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:10,838
That morning's dive was number three hundred and
twenty-five.

23
00:01:10,878 --> 00:01:14,373
Eight hours on the bottom, then up, then back to
the ship.

24
00:01:14,413 --> 00:01:18,031
At nine eighteen the submersible was already on
the surface,

25
00:01:18,071 --> 00:01:21,871
hanging from a crane, waiting for the towline to
be clipped on.

26
00:01:21,911 --> 00:01:24,857
That is when the alarm sounded in the aft
sphere.

27
00:01:24,897 --> 00:01:28,393
The towline had fouled the aft hatch and
wrenched it open.

28
00:01:28,433 --> 00:01:33,635
Water poured in, into a compartment that held
machinery, oil, and a tonne of buoyancy.

29
00:01:33,675 --> 00:01:37,414
Pisces Three heeled over, went upside down, and
began to fall.

30
00:01:37,454 --> 00:01:41,132
Inside, Chapman and Mallinson did the only
things they could.

31
00:01:41,172 --> 00:01:42,290
They cut the power.

32
00:01:42,330 --> 00:01:45,338
They released four hundred pounds of lead
ballast.

33
00:01:45,378 --> 00:01:50,580
They switched off the depth gauge at five
hundred feet, because the glass might burst.

34
00:01:50,620 --> 00:01:53,140
Then they put cushions around their heads,

35
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:57,020
so they would not bite through their own tongues
when they hit.

36
00:01:57,080 --> 00:01:59,844
Thirty seconds later, they hit the bottom.

37
00:01:59,884 --> 00:02:02,247
Four hundred and eighty metres down.

38
00:02:02,287 --> 00:02:07,388
The pressure outside was roughly forty-eight
times what it is at the surface.

39
00:02:07,428 --> 00:02:10,392
And inside, the arithmetic had already begun.

40
00:02:10,432 --> 00:02:14,398
A normal dive carries seventy-two hours of
emergency oxygen.

41
00:02:14,438 --> 00:02:16,468
Eight of those hours were gone.

42
00:02:16,508 --> 00:02:20,340
Sixty-four left, for two men, in a sphere six
feet across,

43
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:22,810
in water four degrees above freezing.

44
00:02:22,850 --> 00:02:27,217
The aft sphere was flooded, so the craft sat
tail-down in the mud.

45
00:02:27,257 --> 00:02:32,023
Every system that drew power was off, which
meant no heat, and no light.

46
00:02:32,063 --> 00:02:35,629
They had one cheese sandwich, and one can of
lemonade.

47
00:02:35,669 --> 00:02:37,832
They did not open either of them.

48
00:02:37,872 --> 00:02:39,568
Not for seventy-six hours.

49
00:02:39,608 --> 00:02:43,039
That decision, and the carbon dioxide scrubbers
they

50
00:02:43,079 --> 00:02:45,950
rationed by hand, is the reason they lived.

51
00:02:46,010 --> 00:02:49,788
There is a detail in this story that most
retellings skip.

52
00:02:49,828 --> 00:02:52,750
Carbon dioxide does not only make you sleepy.

53
00:02:52,790 --> 00:02:54,067
It makes you stupid.

54
00:02:54,107 --> 00:02:57,227
The scrubber fan had to run every forty minutes.

55
00:02:57,267 --> 00:02:58,872
They let it run for less.

56
00:02:58,912 --> 00:03:01,045
They spoke as little as possible.

57
00:03:01,085 --> 00:03:02,229
They barely moved.

58
00:03:02,269 --> 00:03:08,022
When the cold got into their bones, they
squeezed each other's hands instead of talking.

59
00:03:08,062 --> 00:03:11,709
The air went thick and manky, as Mallinson
described it.

60
00:03:11,749 --> 00:03:12,894
Their heads ached.

61
00:03:12,934 --> 00:03:15,724
Their sweat went cold inside their clothes.

62
00:03:15,764 --> 00:03:19,871
Through the porthole, for three days, there was
nothing to see.

63
00:03:19,911 --> 00:03:20,511
No fish.

64
00:03:20,438 --> 00:03:21,188
No daylight.

65
00:03:21,228 --> 00:03:25,796
Just silt, and the occasional scrape of a line
dragging over the hull.

66
00:03:25,836 --> 00:03:30,535
Twice they heard something, and let themselves
believe it was a rescuer.

67
00:03:30,575 --> 00:03:31,589
Twice it wasn't.

68
00:03:31,629 --> 00:03:36,065
On the surface, meanwhile, a very large machine
was being assembled.

69
00:03:36,105 --> 00:03:36,705
Badly.

70
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:39,142
The alarm went out across three navies.

71
00:03:39,182 --> 00:03:43,914
A Canadian coast guard ship, the John Cabot,
turned for the Celtic Sea.

72
00:03:43,954 --> 00:03:47,880
A British survey ship, HMS Hecate, came with
special ropes.

73
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:52,317
From California, the United States Navy loaded a
remote-controlled

74
00:03:52,357 --> 00:03:55,812
recovery vehicle called CURV Three onto an
aircraft.

75
00:03:55,852 --> 00:04:00,249
In between, the only craft that could actually
reach them were two

76
00:04:00,289 --> 00:04:03,476
sister submersibles, Pisces Two and Pisces Five.

77
00:04:03,516 --> 00:04:07,105
Pisces Two went down first, and lost its lifting
line.

78
00:04:07,145 --> 00:04:10,063
Pisces Five found them, and its hook failed.

79
00:04:10,103 --> 00:04:12,752
Pisces Two came back, and took on water.

80
00:04:12,792 --> 00:04:15,979
CURV Three arrived, and had an electrical fault.

81
00:04:16,019 --> 00:04:19,743
The men at the bottom could hear the attempts
happening.

82
00:04:19,783 --> 00:04:21,356
They could not see them.

83
00:04:21,396 --> 00:04:25,995
By the end of the second night, Pisces Five was
ordered up for power,

84
00:04:26,035 --> 00:04:29,530
and they were alone again, with the air going
stale.

85
00:04:29,590 --> 00:04:32,255
At four in the morning on September first,

86
00:04:32,295 --> 00:04:37,986
Pisces Two went down for one last attempt,
carrying a toggle designed for this exact job.

87
00:04:38,026 --> 00:04:38,887
It clipped on.

88
00:04:38,927 --> 00:04:41,205
Then CURV Three fixed a second line.

89
00:04:41,245 --> 00:04:45,777
Only then did Chapman and Mallinson open the
sandwich and the lemonade.

90
00:04:45,817 --> 00:04:48,868
The lift began at ten fifty, and it was violent.

91
00:04:48,908 --> 00:04:52,281
A submersible with a flooded tail, swinging on
lines,

92
00:04:52,321 --> 00:04:55,437
slamming against itself for two and a half
hours.

93
00:04:55,477 --> 00:04:59,751
At a hundred metres it stopped, so divers could
add heavier cables.

94
00:04:59,791 --> 00:05:04,001
At one seventeen in the afternoon, Pisces Three
broke the surface.

95
00:05:04,041 --> 00:05:05,353
The hatch was jammed.

96
00:05:05,393 --> 00:05:07,736
It took another half hour to open it.

97
00:05:07,776 --> 00:05:12,308
Chapman and Mallinson had been inside for
eighty-four and a half hours.

98
00:05:12,348 --> 00:05:16,494
When the cylinder was checked, twelve minutes of
oxygen remained.

99
00:05:16,534 --> 00:05:21,774
They had stretched seventy-two hours of life
support by another twelve and a half,

100
00:05:21,814 --> 00:05:25,316
using nothing but patience, arithmetic, and each
other.

101
00:05:25,356 --> 00:05:27,763
Both men lived for decades afterwards.

102
00:05:27,803 --> 00:05:30,725
They phoned one another every September first.

103
00:05:30,765 --> 00:05:33,945
And every year the ocean repeated the same
lesson.

104
00:05:33,985 --> 00:05:35,490
Depth does not kill you.

105
00:05:35,530 --> 00:05:37,140
Running out of time does.

